1
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,462
Good morning, I'm Whale Girl. Today is October 3.

2
00:00:07,482 --> 00:00:11,112
Seven stories today: Anthropic opens an engineer academy,

3
00:00:11,132 --> 00:00:16,032
Microsoft releases three voice models, ChatGPT gets virtual try-on,

4
00:00:16,052 --> 00:00:20,692
DGX Spark gets a 64GB version, arXiv limits monthly submissions,

5
00:00:20,712 --> 00:00:25,981
Flux 3 Image makes steadier edits, and Cloudflare open-sources decision models.

6
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:27,356
First, on October 2,

7
00:00:27,376 --> 00:00:29,996
Anthropic launched Claude Frontier Academy,

8
00:00:30,016 --> 00:00:33,375
a program for engineers who deploy AI inside companies.

9
00:00:33,395 --> 00:00:36,495
It's putting in $100 million, and by the end of 2027,

10
00:00:36,515 --> 00:00:39,415
it aims to train 10,000 Frontier Deployed Engineers.

11
00:00:39,435 --> 00:00:43,665
It works like a medical residency: a multi-day in-person bootcamp,

12
00:00:43,685 --> 00:00:47,028
then simulated enterprise deployments, then a graded test.

13
00:00:47,048 --> 00:00:51,177
After that, residents lead a real Claude project at their own company,

14
00:00:51,197 --> 00:00:54,457
run it for 12 weeks, then take a final test to get certified.

15
00:00:54,477 --> 00:00:55,926
Participants must be nominated by their company.

16
00:00:55,946 --> 00:01:00,778
First cohort partners include Accenture, Deloitte, McKinsey and Morgan Stanley.

17
00:01:00,798 --> 00:01:05,423
Prior AI agent experience isn't required, but you need to be a hands-on software engineer.

18
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:07,718
Cohorts run in San Francisco, New York and London,

19
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:10,698
and the first engineers are expected in early 2027.

20
00:01:10,718 --> 00:01:13,751
Users in China can only watch for now.

21
00:01:13,951 --> 00:01:18,955
Second, on October 1, Microsoft AI released three voice models at once.

22
00:01:18,975 --> 00:01:23,051
The first is MAI-Transcribe-2-Streaming, a real-time transcription model

23
00:01:23,071 --> 00:01:26,489
for 60 languages that shows text as you speak.

24
00:01:26,509 --> 00:01:29,504
Microsoft says first words appear in just over 100 ms,

25
00:01:29,524 --> 00:01:32,985
and it ranks first for accuracy on Artificial Analysis.

26
00:01:33,005 --> 00:01:37,296
It costs $0.54 per hour, an introductory price through the end of this year.

27
00:01:37,316 --> 00:01:41,346
The other two are text-to-speech models,

28
00:01:41,366 --> 00:01:46,127
MAI-Voice-2.1 and the faster MAI-Voice-2.1-Flash, both covering 23 languages.

29
00:01:46,147 --> 00:01:50,408
Flash has 150 ms end-to-end latency and costs $15 per million characters.

30
00:01:50,428 --> 00:01:52,593
The standard version costs $22.

31
00:01:52,613 --> 00:01:54,873
They can also clone a voice from a few seconds of audio,

32
00:01:54,893 --> 00:01:57,733
with consent safeguards, Microsoft says.

33
00:01:57,753 --> 00:02:00,668
All three are on Microsoft Foundry,

34
00:02:00,688 --> 00:02:04,195
and the two voice models are also on OpenRouter.

35
00:02:04,395 --> 00:02:06,210
Third, on October 1,

36
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:11,031
OpenAI added two shopping features to ChatGPT: virtual try-on and Favorites.

37
00:02:11,051 --> 00:02:14,566
Here's how try-on works: upload a selfie or full-body photo,

38
00:02:14,586 --> 00:02:16,486
tap Try On in shopping results,

39
00:02:16,506 --> 00:02:19,892
and see clothes or accessories on yourself.

40
00:02:19,912 --> 00:02:24,467
You can also upload a product screenshot and ask it to put that item on you.

41
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:27,652
You can describe a style and have it pick a full outfit,

42
00:02:27,672 --> 00:02:30,489
or upload a celebrity photo to find similar items.

43
00:02:30,509 --> 00:02:34,990
Both features are built on the newly launched ChatGPT Images 2.5 model.

44
00:02:35,010 --> 00:02:38,145
Favorites saves products to your Library,

45
00:02:38,165 --> 00:02:41,122
next to your try-on images, so you can come back later.

46
00:02:41,142 --> 00:02:46,471
Both are launching globally. Google launched a similar virtual try-on last year.

47
00:02:46,671 --> 00:02:51,606
Fourth, on October 2, NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of DGX Spark,

48
00:02:51,626 --> 00:02:55,036
a small desktop machine built for running AI.

49
00:02:55,056 --> 00:02:58,352
It starts at $4,999 and goes on sale October 23,

50
00:02:58,372 --> 00:03:02,410
with versions from Acer, ASUS, Dell, HP, MSI and others.

51
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:06,295
It has the same GB10 Grace Blackwell chip as the 128GB model,

52
00:03:06,315 --> 00:03:10,602
just half the memory, and runs models up to 100 billion parameters.

53
00:03:10,622 --> 00:03:14,703
According to IT Home, the 128GB version costs $6,950.

54
00:03:14,723 --> 00:03:18,216
There's also a new cluster assistant: link two 64GB units with a cable

55
00:03:18,236 --> 00:03:22,893
to pool 128GB of memory and run models up to 200 billion parameters.

56
00:03:22,913 --> 00:03:24,754
The assistant detects connected units

57
00:03:24,774 --> 00:03:28,267
and configures the ConnectX-7 network for you.

58
00:03:28,287 --> 00:03:31,602
In NVIDIA's own test with Qwen 3.8 27B,

59
00:03:31,622 --> 00:03:34,704
two units ran up to 1.7x as fast as one.

60
00:03:34,904 --> 00:03:38,039
Fifth, the preprint site arXiv,

61
00:03:38,059 --> 00:03:40,432
starting October 1, is limiting how many papers people can submit.

62
00:03:40,452 --> 00:03:42,292
Each submitter can send at most 2 papers per month,

63
00:03:42,312 --> 00:03:46,250
with at most 3 active submissions at a time, across all categories.

64
00:03:46,270 --> 00:03:49,385
The reason is volume: 40,363 submissions this September,

65
00:03:49,405 --> 00:03:54,911
versus 20,569 in September 2024, nearly double in two years.

66
00:03:54,931 --> 00:03:59,157
Go back further, and September 2016 had just 9,869.

67
00:03:59,177 --> 00:04:03,812
In the cs.AI category alone, submissions grew more than 6x in two years.

68
00:04:03,832 --> 00:04:06,577
arXiv says it's seeing more thin papers,

69
00:04:06,597 --> 00:04:09,317
more single works split into several papers,

70
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:12,477
and more AI-written papers.

71
00:04:12,497 --> 00:04:14,642
Rejected papers count toward the monthly limit.

72
00:04:14,662 --> 00:04:18,659
The limit applies only to the submitter, not co-authors.

73
00:04:18,859 --> 00:04:20,654
Sixth, on October 1,

74
00:04:20,674 --> 00:04:25,011
image model maker Black Forest Labs released Flux 3 Image.

75
00:04:25,031 --> 00:04:28,172
Its strength is stable editing: you can edit step by step,

76
00:04:28,192 --> 00:04:31,567
changing only what you pick and leaving the rest alone.

77
00:04:31,587 --> 00:04:35,602
You lay out the scene with boxes: one box per element with a description,

78
00:04:35,622 --> 00:04:38,537
and later you can edit any single box.

79
00:04:38,557 --> 00:04:41,770
It takes up to 10 reference images and outputs up to 4K.

80
00:04:41,790 --> 00:04:46,969
Around the same time, Ideogram announced version 4.5, also focused on selective edits.

81
00:04:46,989 --> 00:04:51,170
There's a free online demo, and the API is 50% off through October 8.

82
00:04:51,190 --> 00:04:55,656
According to The Decoder, an open-weight version is expected in the coming weeks.

83
00:04:55,856 --> 00:04:57,667
Last one: on October 1,

84
00:04:57,687 --> 00:05:01,753
Cloudflare open-sourced two decision models, Clef and Clef-flash.

85
00:05:01,773 --> 00:05:05,299
Yesterday we covered Amazon's decision model. Models like this don't write text.

86
00:05:05,319 --> 00:05:10,534
They answer yes or no, pick an option, or give a score, with probabilities.

87
00:05:10,554 --> 00:05:16,212
Clef is built on Alibaba's Qwen3.8-27B, and Clef-flash on Qwen3.5-9B.

88
00:05:16,232 --> 00:05:20,572
Cloudflare's median latency: 38.8 ms for Clef-flash,

89
00:05:20,592 --> 00:05:25,232
209.3 ms for Clef, and 524.1 ms for rival Jev.

90
00:05:25,252 --> 00:05:30,481
Both can read images and have a 64K context, twice Jev's 32K.

91
00:05:30,501 --> 00:05:33,761
They use the Apache 2.0 license, with weights on Hugging Face,

92
00:05:33,781 --> 00:05:37,466
and you can call them on Cloudflare Workers AI.

93
00:05:37,486 --> 00:05:40,051
Cloudflare also announced a reinforcement learning fine-tuning service,

94
00:05:40,071 --> 00:05:43,264
with hands-on help from its engineers at first.

95
00:05:43,464 --> 00:05:46,039
That's all for today. Sources are in the description.

96
00:05:46,059 --> 00:05:50,001
The National Day holiday isn't over yet. Get some rest, and see you tomorrow.
